# Bantay Baha Flood Monitor — Sprint Plan

## 1. User Stories and Acceptance Criteria

### User Story 1: Resident

As a resident living near a creek, I want to see the current water level so that I can make informed safety decisions.

Acceptance criteria:

- The dashboard displays the latest water level in centimeters.
- Normal is below 100 cm, Alert is 100–149 cm, and Danger is 150 cm or higher.
- The dashboard refreshes automatically every five seconds.

### User Story 2: Barangay Official

As a barangay official, I want to view recent readings and water-level trends so that I can monitor flood conditions.

Acceptance criteria:

- A line chart displays recent water-level readings.
- A table displays the ten latest readings.
- The dashboard displays the average, minimum, maximum, status counts, and trend.

### User Story 3: Resident Seeking Safety Information

As a resident, I want to receive a bilingual flood advisory so that I know what actions to take.

Acceptance criteria:

- A Generate Advisory button requests an advisory based on the readings.
- The advisory uses Filipino and English and contains no more than three sentences.
- A rule-based fallback appears if the AI service fails.

